    "answer": "Now I have a thorough understanding of the project. Let me frame the task and propose solution options.\n\n---\n\n## Task: split-slow-test-classes-for-parallelism\n\n### Context\n\nThis is a **C# xUnit test project** (VisualRelay.Tests) with ~430 test source files. The project already has `\"parallelizeTestCollections\": true` in `xunit.runner.json`. However, many test classes are very large \u2014 40+ files exceed 10 KB, some exceed 15 KB. In xUnit's default mode, **each test class is its own collection**, and collections run serially with respect to each other. So a single large/slow test class with many `[Fact]` or `[Theory]` methods becomes a bottleneck: all tests inside it must run sequentially, blocking the parallel runner from fully utilising available CPU cores.\n\nThe goal is to **split these oversized/slow test classes** into multiple smaller test classes (or equivalent parallel-friendly units) so the test runner can schedule them in parallel, reducing the overall wall-clock time of the test suite.\n\n### Problem details\n\n- xUnit default: one test collection per class, collections run in parallel with each other, but tests *within* a collection run sequentially.\n- Many test classes have **10\u201318 KB of test code** (e.g., `RelayDriverTests.cs` at 15.5 KB, `MainWindowViewModelTests.cs` at 15 KB, `Installer5Bootstrap3LauncherTests.cs` at 14.7 KB, `BackendConfigGeneratorTests.cs` at 14.2 KB, etc.).\n- Some already use **partial class files** (e.g., `ActivityColumnTabsUiTests` split into `AccordionLayout.cs`, `BackCompat.cs`, `Divider.cs`, etc.) \u2014 but these are still *one class* across multiple files, so still one collection.\n- Other classes (e.g., `CommandGuardDeciderTests`) split into named partials under a subdirectory but remain one class.\n- The test command uses `-m:1` (single MSBuild process) but that doesn't affect xUnit's parallel execution \u2014 the bottleneck is at the collection level.\n\n### Solution Options\n\nHere are **three approaches** to split the slow test classes for parallelism:\n\n---\n\n**Option A \u2014 Use xUnit `[Collection]` attributes to split large classes into multiple collections**\n\nFor each large test class, group related tests into separate inner/nested classes and assign each a different `[Collection]` name (or use no collection so they auto-generate). This way, tests that were previously in one collection can run in parallel. The existing partial-class split files are a natural grouping boundary \u2014 each partial file can become its own collection.\n\n- *Pros*: Minimal structural change; leverages existing file splits; no reorganisation of test logic.\n- *Cons*: Requires adding collection attributes; need to ensure shared state/fixtures are handled correctly (avoid test interference).\n\n**Option B \u2014 Break large classes into separate top-level test classes**\n\nFor each oversized class, extract logical groups of tests into standalone test classes with their own names (e.g., `RelayDriverGitCommitTests` already exists as a separate class \u2014 replicate that pattern for other large classes). Each new class becomes its own collection automatically, enabling parallel execution.\n\n- *Pros*: Clean C# convention; no xUnit attribute magic; automatic parallelism from the class-as-collection rule.\n- *Cons*: More file/class creation; need to extract helper/fixture dependencies into shared code.\n\n**Option C \u2014 Use xUnit `MemberData`/`ClassData` theory parameterisation to collapse many facts into fewer theory methods that run faster**\n\nRather than splitting classes, convert groups of similar `[Fact]` tests into parameterised `[Theory]` tests. This doesn't directly improve parallelism but reduces the number of test methods per collection, indirectly reducing the serial bottleneck while also cutting test execution overhead (test setup/teardown per method).\n\n- *Pros*: Reduces total test count and overhead; can speed up execution even within a single collection.\n- *Cons*: Doesn't address the fundamental parallelism bottleneck; less straightforward for tests with different setup needs.\n\n---\n\n**Recommended starting point**: **Option A** is the most pragmatic \u2014 the partial class files already provide a natural decomposition, and adding `[Collection]` attributes is a surgical change. **Option B** can complement it for classes without existing file splits.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"The test suite has ~430 xUnit test files with many large classes (40+ files >10 KB, some >15 KB) that bottleneck parallelism because xUnit treats each class as a single serial collection. The task is to decompose these oversized/slow test classes so more tests can run in parallel, reducing total wall-clock time.\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"Option A: Use xUnit [Collection] attributes to split large classes into multiple parallel collections, leveraging existing partial-class file groupings.\",\n    \"Option B: Break large classes into separate top-level test classes (one class per logical concern) so each becomes its own parallel collection automatically.\",\n    \"Option C: Convert groups of similar [Fact] methods into parameterised [Theory] methods using MemberData/ClassData to reduce serial test count within a collection.\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
    "exit_code": 0
